                          %mi inf
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sp_*^{inf}
                  to
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-limits"} (∞,1)-limits
                  in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie, def. 1.1.14
            )
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie, remark 1.1.7
            ).
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              This means that a “formal deformation problem” is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
              whose geometric structure is detected by the “test spaces” in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Sp
                      %mo *
                      %mi inf
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sp_*^{inf}
              in a way that respects gluing (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent"}> descent
              ) in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Sp
                      %mo *
                      %mi inf
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sp_*^{inf}
              as given by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimits"} (∞,1)-colimits
              there. The first condition requires that there is an essentially unique such probe by the point, hence that these higher geometric space have essentially a single global point. This is the condition that reflects the infinitesimal nature of the deformation problem.
          %p
            We will often just write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Sp
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sp_*
            for a deformation context
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Sp
                      %mo *
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Sp_*, \{E_\alpha\}_\alpha)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{E_\alpha\}
            are understood.
          #FormalModuliIsPresentableCategory.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
